A mandala is a geometric image of wholeness. The word is Sanskrit for "circle,"
and the form is almost always built the same way: a center, a surrounding
pattern arranged with radial symmetry, and an outer boundary that marks where
the image ends and the world begins. That simple structure has carried an
unusually large amount of meaning. For more than three thousand years, across
cultures that had no contact with one another, people have drawn, painted,
carved, and built mandalas to do one thing above all: to draw the eye and the
mind from the outer edge of experience toward a center, and in doing so, toward
a sense of order and of self.

This article covers where mandala art comes from, what its geometry means
across the traditions that use it, how the symbol traveled into Western art and
psychology, and how to read a mandala as a practice rather than only look at
one as a design.

## Etymology and the earliest mandalas

The term and the image first appear together in India, in the *Rig Veda*, the
oldest of the Hindu scriptures, composed across the Vedic period from roughly
1500 to 500 BCE. The *Rig Veda* is organized into ten books, and each of those
books is itself called a *mandala*. Together they hold 1,028 hymns. The books
are described as mandalas because each one is meant to lead a reader from the
outer rim of ordinary understanding toward a center of meaning, exactly as the
visual form does.

This is worth stating carefully, because it is often simplified. The *Rig Veda*
is the first written appearance of the word and the idea, not necessarily the
origin of the form. Circular images of order are older than any text we have for
them. The soapstone seals of the Indus Valley Civilization, with a central
figure surrounded by enclosed symbols, can be read as mandalas in everything but
name. What the Vedic tradition did was give the form a vocabulary and a purpose:
a self-teaching image that lifts attention from the mundane toward the sublime.

## The grammar of the circle

Across very different traditions, most mandalas share a small visual grammar.
Learning it is most of what it takes to read one.

- **The center** is the seed point. It may hold a deity, a principle, a single
  figure, or simply a point of focus. Everything else in the image is arranged
  in relation to it.
- **Radial symmetry** organizes the surrounding field. Fourfold, eightfold, and
  far higher symmetries are common. The repetition says, in effect, that every
  direction is accounted for and nothing has been left out.
- **The gate or threshold** appears in many traditional mandalas as doorways at
  the cardinal points. A mandala is meant to be entered, not only viewed.
- **The boundary** is the outer ring. It marks the edge between the ordered
  world inside the image and everything outside it. Crossing that boundary, even
  with the eyes, is the beginning of the practice.

A mandala need not be a perfect circle to qualify. As the World History
Encyclopedia notes, the form also takes shape as a square enclosing a circle, a
circle enclosing a star or flower, or a series of nested rings. The Celtic cross
has been read by some scholars as a mandala. The circle is the most common form,
not the only one.

## The mandala across traditions

What makes the mandala remarkable is not any single tradition but the way the
same structure recurs across traditions that developed it independently.

**Hinduism.** In the Hindu vision, the mandala reflects order, the eternal order
the tradition calls *Sanatan Dharma*. The person drawing a mandala in paint,
chalk, sand, or yarn was understood to be receiving the image from a higher
source rather than inventing it freely. The image of the deity Ganesha, the
remover of obstacles, at the center of a mandala is a familiar example: the
observer is invited to travel inward from distraction toward that center.

**Jainism and Buddhism.** Around 600 BCE, a wave of philosophical schools arose
in India questioning the orthodox Hindu view. Among them, Jainism and Buddhism
both adopted the mandala. Jain mandalas often place an enlightened teacher, a
*tirthankara* such as Mahavira, at the center of widening squares, offering the
observer a spiritual map of the path. Buddhist mandalas are more varied still,
and the Tibetan tradition developed them most elaborately. A Tibetan mandala is
a visual representation of the universe and a guide for meditation, with deities
and sacred elements arranged in a precise geometric order. The fourteenth
century "Twenty-three Deity Nairātmyā Mandala" in the Cleveland Museum of Art is
a fine surviving example, its central figure symbolizing selflessness.

The Buddhist sand mandala makes the meaning of the form unusually explicit.
Monks construct an intricate mandala from colored sand over many days, then
deliberately sweep it away. The destruction is the teaching: a meditation on the
impermanence of all things, including the beautiful and the sacred.

## How the mandala traveled west

The mandala did not stay within Asia, and it did not arrive in the West only in
the twentieth century. Circular images of spiritual unity appear independently
in Christian, Islamic, and other traditions. The rose windows of Gothic churches
are the clearest case. A Victorian example, the 1860s rose window at St. Matthias
Church in Richmond, England, is not a copy of a Tibetan mandala, yet its circular
form, radial geometry, and role as a focal point for contemplation echo the same
visual language in a Christian setting.

The mandala also appears on the covers of the Torah and the Quran, in the haloed
saints of Christian iconography, on the patterned ceilings of mosques, and in the
circular hoop of the Native American dreamcatcher. The recurrence is broad enough
that it asks for an explanation.

The most influential modern attempt at one came from the Swiss psychiatrist Carl
Gustav Jung (1875 to 1961). Jung began drawing a small circular image each
morning around 1918, and only gradually understood what he was doing. In his
autobiography *Memories, Dreams, Reflections*, he wrote that the mandala
"is the center. It is the exponent of all paths. It is the path to the center, to
individuation." For Jung, the mandala was a symbol of the self, the whole
personality, and the act of making one was a way to observe and integrate the
psyche. He connected its cross-cultural recurrence to what he called the
collective unconscious, a shared layer of universal images that, in his view,
explained why the same archetypes surface in cultures with no historical contact.

Jung's work opened the door to secular uses of the mandala while he continued to
emphasize its spiritual depth. Through him, and later through Joseph Campbell,
whose ideas drew on Jung, the form became widely known in the West from the
1960s onward. Today the mandala turns up in art therapy, grief counseling, yoga
studios, and the best-selling adult coloring book, valued in each setting for the
same reason: the inward pull of a centered, ordered image.

## A note on borrowing

The mandala's spread raises a real question that an honest account should name.
When a sacred symbol with specific religious meaning becomes a decorative motif
or a wellness product, something is gained and something is risked. The
adaptability that lets the mandala speak across cultures is the same quality that
can flatten it into pattern, detached from the traditions that gave it weight.
There is no tidy resolution here. The most respectful approach is to know what
the form has meant to the people who built it, and to treat that history as
context rather than decoration.

## How to read a mandala

A mandala rewards a different kind of looking than most images. You do not
analyze it so much as travel through it. A simple practice:

1. Rest your eyes first on the outer edge. Notice the boundary, the line between
   the image and everything else.
2. Move inward slowly, ring by ring, letting each layer register without naming
   it or deciding what it means.
3. Arrive at the center and stay there a little longer than is comfortable.
4. Then reverse the journey, carrying whatever the center held back out toward
   the edge.

The reading is the round trip. What you notice on the way out is usually
different from what you noticed on the way in, and that difference is the point.
This is what every tradition that uses the mandala has in common, from the books
of the *Rig Veda* to a Tibetan sand painting to a circle Jung drew in a notebook
before breakfast: the form moves attention from the periphery toward a center, and
in doing so, returns the viewer to themselves.

## Sixty-four mandalas

The Universal Language deck applies this contemplative grammar to a set of
sixty-four painted mandalas, one for each hexagram of the I Ching, and through
the hexagrams, to the Gene Keys and the gates of Human Design. Each painting is a
mandala in the full sense the traditions describe: a center, a symmetry, a
threshold, and a specific quality of energy arranged around the middle. If you
would rather practice the round trip than read about it, the simplest place to
begin is to draw a card and make the journey inward yourself.

